  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nSPC has upgraded to a Slight Risk, level 2 out of 5 risk, for\nmuch of northwest Wisconsin for this afternoon and into the\nearly evening. A Marginal Risk, level 1 out of 5 risk, extends\ninto the I-35 corridor in Minnesota. The biggest question is how\nmuch heating will occur across northwest Wisconsin this \nafternoon with a combination of both low level cloud cover \nbeneath the cap, and anvil spreading across from storms further \nsouth in Minnesota. The longer this cloud cover holds, the less \nbuoyancy will build which will limit the severe thunderstorm \nthreat. If clouds are able to dissipate some, MLCAPE values on \nthe order of 1500-2000 j/kg could materialize across northwest \nWisconsin with surface dewpoints in the mid 60s ahead of the \ncold front. Effective deep layer shear of 25-30kts will be \nsufficient for some organized convective structures. However, \nthis modest shear will place somewhat of a limit on storm \norganization, intensity and the hazards. Primary threats include\nlarge hail to ping pong ball size, damaging winds to 60 mph, \nlocally heavy rainfall and dangerous lightning. The time of the \npeak threat in northwest Wisconsin looks to be between 21Z and \n00Z (4pm-7 pm CDT). \n\nFor the rest of the forecast for this weekend and into next\nweek, quasi zonal flow is favored across the lower 48. This \noverall large scale flow pattern favors near normal temperatures\nwith high temperatures in the 70s to lower 80s. The main storm \ntrack is favored to remain south of the Northland, however, low \npredictability impulses embedded in the faster flow aloft and \ndaytime heating would present a near daily, sporadic coverage of\nrain showers and thunderstorms (isolated to scattered \ncoverage). Overall, with limited buoyancy, severe weather is not\nlikely.",
